verify-sequence-ticket: TPM2_VerifySequenceComplete produces a TPM_ST_MESSAGE_VERIFIED ticket over the message and keeps the sequence on failure

Classification: spec-deviation bug fix.

Specification (TPM 2.0 Library, Part 3 (version 185),
"TPM2_VerifySequenceComplete"):

  "This command is like TPM2_VerifySignature() with the following
  differences: [...] Verifying takes a message (provided via
  TPM2_SequenceUpdate()) rather than a digest. The ticket's tag is
  TPM_ST_MESSAGE_VERIFIED."

Specification (TPM 2.0 Library, Part 2 (version 185), "TPMT_TK_VERIFIED"):

  "HMAC_contextAlg(proof, (tag || digestOrMessage || keyName || metadata))"
  (digestOrMessage: "the signed digest or message"; metadata is empty for
  TPM_ST_MESSAGE_VERIFIED.)

Specification (TPM 2.0 Library, Part 3 (version 185), "Response Codes"):

  "Any other value indicates that the command did not complete and the state
  of the TPM is unchanged."

TPM2_VerifySequenceComplete returns a TPM_ST_VERIFIED ticket over the digest
of the message for a scheme that signs a digest, and a NULL TPM_ST_VERIFIED
ticket for the HMAC and ML-DSA schemes. It also ends the running hash or HMAC
of the sequence in place, so after a failed verification (TPM_RC_SIGNATURE)
the sequence object stays loaded with a consumed state. The ticket is now a
running HMAC alongside the sequence: TPM2_VerifySequenceStart obtains the
proof of the key's hierarchy (before the sequence object is allocated, so its
error leaves the TPM unchanged) and starts HMAC_contextAlg(proof, ...) with
the tag (the running HMAC is kept in the 'state' union after the running
state of the message, because a SEQUENCE_OBJECT has to fit in an OBJECT slot,
and is exported/imported by a context save/load; its hierarchy is a new
field before 'state'), TPM2_SequenceUpdate adds the message to it, and TPM2_VerifySequenceComplete adds the key's Name and returns
<TPM_ST_MESSAGE_VERIFIED, hierarchy, HMAC> for every scheme, or
<TPM_ST_MESSAGE_VERIFIED, TPM_RH_NULL, Empty Buffer> for a key in TPM_RH_NULL
or with nameAlg TPM_ALG_NULL. The running state of the message (hash, HMAC,
or ML-DSA through its context export/import) is completed on a copy, so a
failed verification leaves the sequence unchanged. As in tpm-rs.
